## Setup

Index cells by $(i,j)$ with $0 \le i,j \le 4$. Let $x_{i,j}$ be the number of pulses applied to node $(i,j)$. The final clearance at $(i,j)$ is
$$
(Ax)_{i,j} \;=\; x_{i,j} + \sum_{(i',j')\sim(i,j)} x_{i',j'},
$$
where $\sim$ denotes orthogonal adjacency. We need $Ax = s\mathbf{1}$ with $x_{i,j}\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$ and $s$ a positive integer, and we seek the minimal such $s$.

Here $A = I + \operatorname{Adj}(P_5\boxtimes P_5)$ (self-loops plus grid adjacency).

## Necessity: $11 \mid s$

The path $P_5$ has adjacency eigenvalues $2\cos(k\pi/6)\in\{\sqrt3,1,0,-1,-\sqrt3\}$ with eigenvectors
$$
u = (1,0,-1,0,1)\quad(\text{eigenvalue }0),\qquad
v = (1,-1,0,1,-1)\quad(\text{eigenvalue }-1).
$$
For the $5\times5$ grid, $A = I + \operatorname{Adj}$ has eigenvalue $1+\lambda_i+\lambda_j$. This equals $0$ exactly when $(\lambda_i,\lambda_j)\in\{(0,-1),(-1,0)\}$, so $\ker A$ is **2-dimensional**, spanned by
$$
N_1 = u\otimes v,\qquad N_2 = v\otimes u.
$$
(Indeed $\operatorname{Adj}(u\otimes v)=(\operatorname{Adj}u)\otimes v+u\otimes(\operatorname{Adj}v)=0\otimes v+u\otimes(-v)=-u\otimes v$, so $A N_1 = (I+\operatorname{Adj})N_1 = 0$, and similarly $AN_2=0$.)

Since $\ker A$ is exactly 2-dimensional, **every** solution of $Ax=s\mathbf 1$ differs from any fixed solution by an element of $\ker A$. Now evaluate at cell $(1,1)$:
$$
N_1[1,1]=u[1]\,v[1]=0\cdot(-1)=0,\qquad N_2[1,1]=v[1]\,u[1]=(-1)\cdot0=0.
$$
So $x_{1,1}$ is **invariant** across all solutions. To compute it, use the $D_4$-symmetric solution. By symmetry there are six orbit-types with values $(a,b,c,d,e,f)$ at positions $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2)$ (relative to center). Solving the six linear equations yields
$$
(a,b,c,d,e,f) = (10t,\,3t,\,2t,\,5t,\,7t,\,8t),\qquad s=22t,
$$
so $x_{1,1}=c=2t=s/11$. By invariance, **every** solution satisfies $x_{1,1}=s/11$.

Since $x_{1,1}$ must be a nonneg integer, $11\mid s$. With $s>0$, this forces $s\ge 11$.

## Construction: $s=11$ is achievable

Take the symmetric particular solution at $s=11$ (i.e. $t=1/2$, giving half-integer values) and add $\tfrac12 N_1+\tfrac12 N_2$. Setting $\alpha=\beta=\tfrac12$ in
